This project incorporates and adapts portions of pi-compaction-model:
https://github.com/JMHSV/pi-compaction-model
Copyright (c) 2026 JMHSV, licensed under the MIT License.

The native compaction integration and file-operation restoration logic are derived
from that project. Routing, fallback, validation, diagnostics, and post-compaction
resume behavior are additional work in pi-compaction-router.

This project also adapts portions of pi-openai-server-compaction:
https://github.com/algal/pi-openai-server-compaction
Copyright (c) 2026 Alexis Gallagher, licensed under the MIT License.
Taken from PR #7 head 9ea79cb89fecca4c7ab09739530ee78a654cd32f (refs/pull/7/head),
read at that commit -- not from a published tarball.

The pending-warning stash in src/pending-warning.ts, and the reason it exists -- that a
before-compaction ui.notify is destroyed by the post-compaction chat rebuild, so an
operator-visible warning has to be re-emitted afterwards as a ui.setWidget widget -- are
adapted from that project's src/state.ts and src/index.ts. The finding was re-verified
against this project's own pinned @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent 0.81.1 rather than
adopted on the upstream author's word. Nothing else from that project is used here: its
WebSocket transport, provider takeover, Codex identity headers and remote-compaction
protocol are all out of scope for a router.

This project also adapts portions of Accordion:
https://github.com/a-Fig/Accordion
Copyright (c) 2026 Accordion contributors, licensed under the MIT License.
Read at commit dc037bcdc2929b15bf8e3df2875260d596e258ea -- a clone of the repository at
that commit, never the published @a-fig/accordion tarball, which predates this code and
does not contain it at all.

The compaction ledger's closed outcome taxonomy in src/ledger.ts -- the LedgerOutcome
union and the "never silent" rule that every committed compaction gets a row naming
exactly one cause, including the `unobserved` case for a compaction our own hook never
resolved -- is adapted from that project's extension/accordion.ts:385-457
(`recordPlanOutcome` and its `PlanOutcomeCause` union). The cause names themselves are
not reused: upstream's describe a GUI round-trip, ours describe route resolution.
Upstream counts causes in memory and acknowledges them over a WebSocket; this project
persists a row per compaction instead. Nothing else from Accordion is used here -- its
fidelity ladder, fold/digest engine, Tauri GUI, WebSocket transport and `context`-hook
interception are all out of scope for a router.

This project also adapts portions of AFT:
https://github.com/cortexkit/aft
Copyright (c) 2025 Ufuk Altinok, licensed under the MIT License.
Read at commit 566bcde25cbb2c24f09fdc5e4840a58a65a97677.

Two mechanisms in src/ledger.ts are taken from that project:

  - The tokenize-cost cap and its honest skip flag, from
    crates/aft/src/bash_background/registry.rs:64 (TOKENIZE_CAP_BYTES_PER_STREAM =
    128 * 1024) and :4438-4452 (CompletionTokenCounts::skipped()). The constant keeps
    upstream's value and the discriminated null-count-plus-flag shape is upstream's, as is
    the rule it encodes: never fabricate a count when you declined to compute one.
  - The savings telemetry and its status surface, from
    packages/aft-bridge/src/format.ts:24-28 (compressionSavingsPercent, including both
    edge cases upstream's own tests pin -- a zero original returns null, a negative saving
    clamps to zero), packages/aft-bridge/src/protocol.ts:43-53 (the two-scope
    session/project aggregate shape) and packages/pi-plugin/src/dialogs/status-dialog.ts:
    317-341 (the rendered scope-header-plus-stat-lines layout).

AFT's vendored Rust Claude tokenizer (crates/aft-tokenizer/) is deliberately NOT taken;
docs/estimator-upgrade-decision.md records that decision and what a future vendoring
would owe. AFT's type-erasing `pi.on` casts are recorded as an anti-pattern and avoided.

This project also adapts portions of ai-memory:
https://github.com/akitaonrails/ai-memory
Copyright (c) 2026 Fabio Akita, licensed under the MIT License.
Read at commit e714d992993b5ee76a76e09e64ac5cb441e14aed -- a clone of the repository, never
`npm:ai-memory`, which is an unrelated 1.0.0 stub with no repository field.

The passive provider-health record in src/provider-health.ts is adapted from that project's
crates/ai-memory-llm/src/health.rs, whose module documentation states the rule this file
holds to: "These wrappers never probe providers on their own. They only record the result
of calls the server was already going to make." Taken with it: the status ladder, the
last-call / last-success / last-error triple, error-message truncation at a named constant,
the rule that a success clears the previous error rather than leaving a stale one beside it,
and the snapshot-as-wire-format split. Upstream's provider-role decorator becomes a
per-target record here, because a route chain is a list of targets rather than a pair of
roles. Upstream's daemon substrate, embedding provider, consolidation prompts and eval
harness are not used.
